  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A thorn hedge once cut India clean in half. Four thousand kilometres of it, ordered joined up in 1869, Himalayas down to the Bay of Bengal.",
        "visual_query": "north indian plains landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "open plains"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was there to tax salt. Indian plum, babool, prickly pear, grown nearly four metres high and four metres thick in places.",
        "visual_query": "indian salt flats harvest",
        "visual_fallback": "salt flats"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Over fourteen thousand men patrolled it at its peak, through more than seventeen hundred customs posts. All that, to stop smuggled salt.",
        "visual_query": "dry indian thorny scrubland",
        "visual_fallback": "thorn bushes"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then on 1 April 1879, they dropped it. And a barrier the size of a country simply fell out of the history books.",
        "visual_query": "colonial indian countryside railway",
        "visual_fallback": "old railway"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1998 a researcher named Roy Moxham found what's left: one low earth embankment in Etawah district.",
        "visual_query": "uttar pradesh rural embankment",
        "visual_fallback": "earth embankment"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
